{:edb/db-before-t 2007 :edb/db-after-t 2008 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535320 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:58:40.279Z" 13194139535320 true] [17592186175852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343578 13194139535320 true] [17592186175852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343599 13194139535320 true] [17592186175852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343606 13194139535320 true] [17592186175854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343585 13194139535320 true] [17592186175854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343592 13194139535320 true] [17592186343578 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343579 13194139535320 true] [17592186343578 1089 "q-140385" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343578 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535320 true] [17592186343578 1100 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343578 1113 #uuid "54f8d78f-9334-58cc-8d51-450e53ee811c" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343578 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+4\\text{i} & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(2+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343579 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343580 13194139535320 true] [17592186343579 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343581 13194139535320 true] [17592186343579 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343582 13194139535320 true] [17592186343579 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343583 13194139535320 true] [17592186343579 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343584 13194139535320 true] [17592186343579 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535320 true] [17592186343579 1041 "selection" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343579 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343580 13194139535320 true] [17592186343579 1110 #uuid "285c6789-0ea2-580c-8c00-303d243386d5" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343580 1034 "1+2\\text{i}" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343580 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535320 true] [17592186343580 1111 #uuid "b54a1906-4418-558c-9b9c-70f6e198cd44" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343581 1034 "-1-2\\text{i}" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343581 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535320 true] [17592186343581 1111 #uuid "0b44b287-f452-5531-9f51-50420157ee60" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343582 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535320 true] [17592186343582 1111 #uuid "9845e8b3-3a41-5521-88bc-2a40aafd1faf" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343583 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343583 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535320 true] [17592186343583 1111 #uuid "a6dbe8dd-0ad3-5585-a145-aa06f0e310bf" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343584 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343584 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535320 true] [17592186343584 1111 #uuid "7cd60022-6510-51e0-ae95-18db32723735" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343585 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343586 13194139535320 true] [17592186343585 1089 "q-140388" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343585 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535320 true] [17592186343585 1100 "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343585 1113 #uuid "8ae540d5-f0cb-5097-abf0-3d9578dec947" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343585 1145 "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(4)(-5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -5 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(2+4\\text{i})(2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343586 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343587 13194139535320 true] [17592186343586 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343588 13194139535320 true] [17592186343586 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343589 13194139535320 true] [17592186343586 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343590 13194139535320 true] [17592186343586 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343591 13194139535320 true] [17592186343586 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535320 true] [17592186343586 1041 "selection" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343586 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343591 13194139535320 true] [17592186343586 1110 #uuid "a9bbfec4-3eb6-5ea8-89d6-d25e88cedc12" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343587 1034 "\\frac{25}{16}" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343587 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535320 true] [17592186343587 1111 #uuid "df618613-22e4-564d-ae8a-9c0689e03728" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343588 1034 "\\frac{25}{9}" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343588 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 131941