-4}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 2\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm \\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5-\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 \\\\ -5 & 12\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7+\\text{i} & 10 \\\\ -5 & 7+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-7+\\text{i}){x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(7+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5-2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(7+\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(7+\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}7+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5+\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}7-\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(7+\\text{i})(7-\\text{i})}{(5)(5)}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535320 true] [17592186343593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343594 13194139535320 true] [17592186343593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343595 13194139535320 true] [17592186343593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343596 13194139535320 true] [17592186343593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343597 13194139535320 true] [175921863